    <p>These three philosophies offer profound, historically tested methods for dealing with the human condition and overcoming suffering. While they all ultimately point toward inner peace and liberation from external circumstances, their maps of the human mind—and what to do with it—differ significantly.</p>

    <p>Here is a breakdown of Stoicism, Zen Buddhism, and how they align with Ramana Maharshi’s method of Self-Enquiry.</p>

    <h2>The Core Philosophies</h2>

    <h3>1. Stoicism: The Mastery of the Mind</h3>
    <p>Originating in ancient Greece and Rome, Stoicism is a philosophy of practical ethics driven by logic and reason (<em>Logos</em>).</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The Core Premise:</strong> Suffering doesn't come from events, but from our judgments about them.</li>
        <li><strong>The Method:</strong> The "Dichotomy of Control." You relentlessly separate what is in your control (your thoughts, actions, and character) from what is not (other people, the weather, disease, reputation) and attach your well-being only to the former.</li>
        <li><strong>The View of the Ego:</strong> The ego is real and necessary. The goal is not to destroy it, but to perfect it through virtue and rational discipline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Zen Buddhism: The Dissolution of the Conceptual Mind</h3>
    <p>Rooted in Mahayana Buddhism and developed in China and Japan, Zen bypasses intellectual philosophy in favor of direct, unmediated experience.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The Core Premise:</strong> Suffering arises from attachment and the illusion of a separate, permanent self (<em>Anatta</em> or No-Self). The conceptual mind traps us in a distorted version of reality.</li>
        <li><strong>The Method:</strong> <em>Zazen</em> (seated meditation). You sit and observe reality exactly as it is, without labels, judgments, or intellectual analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>The View of the Ego:</strong> The ego is a hallucination. There is no permanent "you" inside your head; there is only the ever-changing flow of present experience.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. Ramana Maharshi: Transcending the Ego via the "I"</h3>
    <p>Ramana Maharshi was a 20th-century Indian sage representing Advaita Vedanta (non-dualism). His primary teaching was <em>Atma Vichara</em>, or Self-Enquiry.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The Core Premise:</strong> All human problems stem from the "I-thought"—the fundamental assumption that you are a separate individual tied to a physical body and mind.</li>
        <li><strong>The Method:</strong> The continuous internal question, <strong>"Who am I?"</strong> This is not a psychological question meant to find an answer like "I am a teacher" or "I am a soul." It is a tool used like a laser. Whenever a thought arises, you ask, "To whom does this thought arise? To me. Who am I?"</li>
        <li><strong>The View of the Ego:</strong> The ego is a phantom. By intensely focusing on the source of the "I" feeling, the ego eventually collapses, revealing the true Self (pure, formless consciousness).</li>
    </ul>

