    <h2>How Stoicism and Zen Align with "Who am I?"</h2>

    <p>If we use Ramana Maharshi's Self-Enquiry as the anchor, here is how the other two philosophies align:</p>

    <h3>Stoicism: A Useful (But Limited) Prerequisite</h3>
    <p>From Maharshi's perspective, Stoicism operates entirely within the realm of the illusion. Stoicism uses the mind to fix the mind. It relies heavily on the "I" (the ego) to assert control over emotions and reactions.</p>
    <p>Maharshi would likely view Stoicism as an excellent tool for developing a purified, calm, and concentrated mind (<em>Sattva</em>), which is highly beneficial for spiritual practice. However, he would argue that Stoicism stops too early. Once the Stoic has built a perfectly disciplined, rational "I," Maharshi would ask the Stoic: <em>"Who is the 'I' that is being so rational?"</em> Stoicism trims the branches of the ego; Maharshi wants to pull it out by the root.</p>

    <h3>Zen Buddhism: Very Close Cousins</h3>
    <p>Zen and Maharshi's teachings are practically neighbors, though they use different vocabularies to describe the same address. Both aim for the complete dissolution of the conceptual mind and the ego.</p>
    <p>The primary difference is linguistic and methodological. Zen emphasizes <strong>Emptiness</strong> and <strong>No-Self</strong>. It tells you to drop the subject ("I") completely and just experience reality. Maharshi emphasizes the <strong>Ultimate Self</strong>. Instead of dropping the "I," he instructs you to grab onto the "I" so fiercely and exclusively that everything else burns away, until the "I" itself dissolves into pure Awareness.</p>
    <p>Ultimately, both Zen's "Emptiness" and Maharshi's "True Self" point to the exact same state: non-dual awareness, free from the illusion of a separate ego.</p>

